@meta
id: 13-03
title: A number where a name should be
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-classes
par: 62
minutes: 6
vscode: read every line, find the three literals by eye
open: retry.config.ts

@brief
Every value in this config is supposed to come from a named constant.
Three of them are still bare numbers, left over from before the
constants file existed. Replace each literal with its constant, in the
order they appear in the file:

    the timeout      →  TIMEOUT_MS
    the attempts     →  MAX_ATTEMPTS
    the backoff      →  BACKOFF_MS

You do not need to know what the numbers are, and that is the point.
Searching for `30000` works for the first one and tells you nothing
about the other two. There are also digits in the queue names and the
hash algorithm, so searching for a digit on its own stops in the wrong
places.

What you want to say is "a colon, a space, and then some digit". A
pattern has a word for "some digit".

@keys
| `\d` | any digit, `0` to `9` |
| `\D` | anything that is not a digit |
| `/pattern/e` `Enter` | land on the last character of the match — here, the digit |
| `n` | next match, same pattern, same offset |
| `cw` | change to the end of the word |

@hints
Describe the shape of a literal value rather than the value itself. All
three look the same from the left: the key, a colon, a space, a digit.
--
`\d` stands for any single digit. `/: \d` finds a colon, a space and a
digit, wherever the digit is on its own rather than inside a name. A
search lands on the *start* of its match, which here is the colon, so
you will want the `/e` offset from chapter 12 to land on the digit.
--
`/: \d/e` `Enter`, then `cwTIMEOUT_MS` and `Esc`. `n` jumps to the next
literal, keeping the offset, and `cw` again — the pattern is the same
each time even though the numbers are not.

@solution
/: \d/e<Enter>  cwTIMEOUT_MS<Esc>  n  cwMAX_ATTEMPTS<Esc>  n  cwBACKOFF_MS<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Fifty-five keystrokes, forty of them the three constant names. The
search was typed once.

`\d` is the first of vim's character classes: a backslash and a letter
that stands for a whole set of characters. The ones you will use are

    \d   a digit                 \D   not a digit
    \w   a word character        \W   not a word character
    \s   whitespace              \S   not whitespace

where "word character" means letters, digits and underscore — the same
definition `w` and `*` use. The capital is always the complement.

Notice why `/: \d` beat `/\d`. On its own, `\d` matched the `2` in
`v2` and the `256` in `sha256` first. The colon and space are context:
they say where the digit has to sit. A pattern is nearly always a bit of
context plus a bit of shape, and getting the context right is what
stops it landing on the wrong thing.

The `/e` offset matters more than it looks. The match was three
characters long — colon, space, digit — and without the offset the
cursor sits on the colon, so `cw` would have eaten the colon instead of
the number. Landing on the end of the match put you on the digit. Later
in the chapter there is a cleaner way to say "match this, but put me
there", and it is one of the best things vim's patterns have.

`cw` on a number changes the whole number, because a run of digits is a
word. That is why the three edits could share one shape — you never had
to know how long each literal was.

@start retry.config.ts
import { BACKOFF_MS, MAX_ATTEMPTS, TIMEOUT_MS } from './retry.constants';

export const retryConfig = {
  queue: 'payments.refunds.v2',
  deadLetter: 'payments.refunds.v2.dead',
  exchange: 'payments.v2',
  routingKey: 'refund.retry',
  signature: 'sha256',
  timeoutMs: 30000,
  consumerTag: 'order-service-v2',
  prefetch: PREFETCH,
  maxAttempts: 5,
  jitter: JITTER,
  backoffMs: 250,
  metricsPrefix: 'p95',
};

@expected retry.config.ts
import { BACKOFF_MS, MAX_ATTEMPTS, TIMEOUT_MS } from './retry.constants';

export const retryConfig = {
  queue: 'payments.refunds.v2',
  deadLetter: 'payments.refunds.v2.dead',
  exchange: 'payments.v2',
  routingKey: 'refund.retry',
  signature: 'sha256',
  timeoutMs: TIMEOUT_MS,
  consumerTag: 'order-service-v2',
  prefetch: PREFETCH,
  maxAttempts: MAX_ATTEMPTS,
  jitter: JITTER,
  backoffMs: BACKOFF_MS,
  metricsPrefix: 'p95',
};
